WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:01.880
[music]

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:10.500
When should you start the story of this painting?

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:13.300
Here in 1910 or

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:16.000
here in 1844? Maybe

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.100
here in Cezanne's studio
at the close of the 19th century,

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:23.200
or maybe the only place you can
really find the genesis of this canvas

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:27.000
is in Jackson Pollock's mind the day he began to drip.

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:30.000
I'm drawn to ask these questions because this painting,

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.300
"One: Number 13 [31], 1950"
is the only abstract work of art that has ever

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:37.400
floored me in person.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:40.500
As soon as my eyes caught it,
it made me feel something.

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:45.700
I don't know that I could describe the feeling, but
I didn't have to go searching for it either,

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:48.600
a problem I often have with non-figurative paintings,

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.600
where I'm standing in front of a canvas,
trying to figure out the reaction

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:55.709
I'm having to it, feeling guilty because nothing's coming.

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:59.900
I don't think the power of this Pollock
depends on its place in the history of art.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:04.700
Its style, its use of color, its hyperactivity
are intrinsic qualities.

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:09.779
But I do think the history of art has
a lot to say through Pollock's drip paintings

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:12.900
which he'd been making
by this point for about four years.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.900
In many ways, they're 
the culmination of something that has

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:19.200
a foggy beginning about a century or two before,

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:22.800
with the gradual end of
church and noble patronage of the Arts

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:26.500
and the dawn of painters painting
what was important to them.

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:29.800
There's no fixed point where you
could mark the beginning of modern art.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.200
Some find it in this painting by Manet from 1863

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:38.700
with its flattened image and scandalous subject
that flouted the values of the Paris salon.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.600
And I think it's as good a moment as any.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:45.700
That year, fully 2/3 of
submissions to the salon were rejected,

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:51.100
and after a public outcry, Emperor Napoleon the third authorized a second salon to feature them

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:54.260
called, appropriately, the "Exhibition of Rejects".

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:56.500
It was a massively popular event.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:59.400
And though these paintings were
criticized as much as they were praised,

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:05.600
it marked a moment of legitimacy for art
that stood outside the Academy's hierarchy of quality.

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:09.300
Once the cat was out of the bag,
things started moving very quickly.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:13.700
Less than ten years later, Claude Monet
and his compatriots launched into impressionism.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:18.600
Concerning themselves not with the objects
they see in the world, but how the light plays off them.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:23.200
Impressionism in turn starts
a chain reaction of, well, reaction.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:27.500
The post-impressionists begin to dispense
with the effects of light and the reality of color,

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:30.789
and paint according to
subjective experience and emotional vision

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:35.300
Then the fauvists take the
arbitrariness of color to kaleidoscopic extremes

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:37.000
in the first decade of the 20th century.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:41.800
I mean, just look at the transformation
that's happened to painting in just 40 short years.

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:46.700
Art has unravelled.
Its centuries long aim of reproducing the physical world

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:50.500
in perspective, color, and form
is rapidly being abandoned.

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:54.800
Wassily Kandinsky, influenced by
the impressionists, by the fauvists, by music,

00:02:54.800 --> 00:03:00.600
and by the geometric spirituality of theosophy
starts pulling away from representation entirely.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.200
In a groundbreaking book, he writes,

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:08.200
"The more abstract is form,
the more clear and direct its appeal." And in 1910,

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:13.000
he paints this, in which
no objects from our world can be recognized at all.

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:15.700
It's the first fully abstract painting.

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:20.920
Except it turns out that Hilma af Clint was actually painting abstracts at least three years prior to this

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:23.170
but no one knew that until many years later.

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:27.400
Meanwhile, Pablo Picasso and Georges Braque
were shaking up painting in their own way,

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:32.500
by formalizing Cezanne's
experiments with multiple perspectives into "cubism".

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:37.300
The Cubists used geometry to
show reality from many angles simultaneously.

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:42.400
Kandinsky takes inspiration from this,
but is more interested in abstracting geometry

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:44.500
from its representational source.

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:49.000
The sparks that fly from these
investigations create several other movements:

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:50.280
"Futurism" in Italy,

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:51.900
"De Stijl" in the Netherlands,

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:54.300
"Suprematism", then "Constructivism" in Russia,

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:56.700
"Dada" in Switzerland, and eventually

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:59.520
"Surrealism" in the 1920s and 1930s.

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:04.600
A key aspect of the surrealist manifesto
is the focus on automatic creation:

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:09.200
writing or speaking without forethought
as a way to access the unconscious mind.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:12.200
Painters like Andre Masson
applied this to the visual arts,

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:16.900
letting ink run free across paper
creating fluid dreamlike drawings

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:19.100
prefiguring the work of Jackson Pollock.

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:24.380
As World War Two ravaged Europe,
several of the Surrealists and other European artists

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:29.200
escaped to New York City and began to
mix with a new school of American painters.

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:33.700
The Americans synthesized and
reacted to all the various European styles.

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:37.300
The "Abstract Expressionists",
as they were called, made work that was

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:40.900
deeply personal, formally inventive, and confrontational.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:43.300
Exiled surrealist Wolfgang Paalen put it best:

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:57.900
Pollock's drip paintings push
abstraction to its furthest limit.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:01.900
Even in Kandinsky, lines still bound recognizable shapes.

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:04.100
In Pollock, there are no shapes;

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:05.900
no suggestions to grab onto.

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:09.400
The line itself dancing across the canvas is set free,

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:11.400
containing nothing, separating nothing.

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:14.939
There is no inside, no outside, 
nowhere for your eyes to rest.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:17.500
All that remains is energy.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:20.200
Wild, emotional energy that peers

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:22.820
into you, not the other way around.

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:27.200
I think that's why I
reacted so strongly to this painting in  person.

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:29.300
I could feel it looking at me.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:33.900
You know, like I said before these
drip paintings have intrinsic value.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:38.000
But I also think they
speak to something deep in human nature,

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:40.160
that we are...obsessive.

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:43.900
In the same way that we won't
stop until we're flying among the stars,

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:45.900
it took less than a hundred years

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:48.200
for this to become

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:49.100
this.

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:52.500
A fast changing world contributed hugely, of course.

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:58.700
But beyond that I do believe there's a drive in us,
to take things as far as they can go,

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:02.500
and this century of modern art 
is an exhilarating example of that.

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:07.480
It's just inspiring how
irrepressible human creativity can be.

